window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"11-05","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"11-05-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-01/","title_he":"א – מעמד הנישואין","title_en":"1. The Occasion of Marriage","body_en":"<p>The wedding day is the great and important day in the lives of the couple. On this day they will begin to fulfil completely the mitzva that is a great principle of the Torah – \"You shall love your fellow as yourself\" – which only within marriage can be realised completely. On this holy day the image of God in them shines, and they break through the barrier of their private personality and make between them a covenant of love and faithfulness; a spark of the Divine unity is revealed between them, and out of this they merit to be partners in the continuation of the process of creation and to bring life and souls into the world.</p>\n<p>It is hard for the heart to contain the greatness of the wondrous and long journey that the groom and bride begin to travel as they enter the ḥuppa. An endless journey to the peaks of love and the depths of responsibility. On their way they will pass through hard hours and happy hours, and many days of blessed routine. On their way they will pass through hours in which their love will blaze up in a storm, and days in which they will express brotherhood and friendship in countless gestures and deeds. According to the greatness of the value of the journey, so great are the difficulties and pitfalls it will set in their way. The more they merit to cleave to the mitzvot of the Torah and to the fences that the Sages established, the easier it will be for them to withstand the tests, and to go on discovering further layers and treasures in the wondrous bond of marriage between them. From this marriage, with the help of Hashem, children will grow up who one day will also enter their ḥuppa and begin their own wondrous journey, from which their grandchildren and great-grandchildren will be born, to the end of all generations.</p>\n<p>Were the groom and bride to see all this at the time of their ḥuppa, they would surely faint, for the heart cannot contain the greatness of the holiness and the responsibility. And here comes their first test: will they turn the wedding into an outward display of pride full of tensions and quarrels, or will they, in humility and modesty, rejoice with a true joy of mitzva with the members of the family and their friends? The Sages said that on the wedding day the sins of the groom and bride are forgiven (see Yerushalmi Bikkurim 3:3). For on this day they want with all their heart to begin their new life in a perfect way: to gladden each other in faithfulness, to honour their parents, to raise and educate children, to keep Shabbatot and festivals, to do kindness, and to be good in the eyes of God and man. To strengthen this positive process it is fitting for the groom and bride to repent on their wedding day, and to pray to Hashem that He help them realise all their good aspirations.</p>\n<p>We learned about the laws of kiddushin and the ketuba in the previous chapter. Now we will learn about how they fit into the order of the wedding, together with the other laws and customs of the wedding.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-02/","title_he":"ב – סדר החתונה – הקידושין","title_en":"2. The Order of the Wedding – Kiddushin","body_en":"<p>The wedding is made up of three stages: a) erusin, which is carried out by kiddushin; b) the ketuba; c) nisu'in, which is carried out by the ḥuppa.</p>\n<p>The Sages instituted that the kiddushin be held after a blessing over a cup of wine. The blessing of kiddushin is intended to express the meaning of kiddushin, and the blessing over the wine expresses the joy in the spreading of the idea of the holiness of betrothal from the upper spiritual worlds down to the physical world. For wine expresses the simple and natural joy of this world of ours. And there is in this also a prayer for the new couple, that the spiritual values of holiness and faithfulness will accompany them all the days of their lives together with the natural feelings of love, joy and gladness, which are expressed in wine.</p>\n<p>The rabbi who conducts the kiddushin takes the cup of wine in his hand and recites two blessings: a) \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who creates the fruit of the vine.\" b) \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us concerning forbidden relations, and forbidden to us those who are betrothed, and permitted to us those who are married to us by ḥuppa and kiddushin. Blessed are You, Hashem, who sanctifies His people Israel by ḥuppa and kiddushin\" (the Yemenite text differs somewhat).</p>\n<p>After the conclusion of the blessing they taste the wine in this order: the rabbi who conducts the kiddushin gives the cup to the groom, and the groom drinks a little. After this the rabbi takes the cup from the groom and hands it to the bride's mother, and she gives it to the bride to taste. The groom does not himself hand the cup to the bride, because they are still at the stage of betrothal.<sup>1</sup> After the groom and bride have drunk from the cup, it is good that the parents or other people drink the wine remaining in the cup; and if those drinking did not intend at the time of the blessing to drink from the cup, they should recite 'ha-gefen' before they drink (see Otzar Ha-Poskim 34:13:3; Nisu'in Ke-Hilkhatam 10:41).</p>\n<p>Regarding the basis of the blessing of kiddushin two views are brought. In the view of the Rambam, just as before many mitzvot the Sages instituted reciting a blessing, so too before the mitzva of kiddushin they instituted a blessing (Hilkhot Ishut 3:23). And in the view of the Rosh, this blessing is not among the blessings over mitzvot but among the blessings of praise and thanksgiving to Hashem, for having distinguished us from the nations and sanctified us with His mitzvot by giving us Divine guidance on how to enter the covenant of marriage (Ketubot 1:12).</p>\n<p>After the conclusion of the preparations with the blessings and drinking the wine, one comes to the kiddushin themselves: the rabbi ensures that the ring belongs to the groom and that the valid witnesses are present and see everything; he asks the bride whether she is willing to be betrothed and to hold out her finger; and then the groom says: \"Behold, you are betrothed to me with this ring according to the law of Moshe and Israel,\" and places the ring on the bride's finger (above 4:3-5).</p>\n<p>From this moment the bride is betrothed and engaged; that is, according to the Torah she has become forbidden to the whole world, with the law of a betrothed maiden, but they are still forbidden to live together as man and wife, because they have not yet married.</p>\n<p>In order that the groom and bride may pass to the stage of nisu'in by the ḥuppa, the groom must give the bride the ketuba, that is, undertake to care faithfully for all her needs, and that if, Heaven forbid, they divorce or he dies before her, she will receive compensation in the sum stated in the ketuba (as explained above 4:6-8).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"According to the Sephardi custom the rabbi drinks from the wine first, since he recited the blessing over the vine, and after him the groom and bride drink. According to the Ashkenazi custom the rabbi does not drink from the wine; rather the blessing he recites over the vine is for the groom and bride."}]},{"slug":"11-05-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-03/","title_he":"ג – חופה – נישואין","title_en":"3. Ḥuppa – Nisu'in","body_en":"<p>After the kiddushin have been held by giving the ring, and after the groom has given his bride the ketuba, one can advance to the stage of nisu'in, from which point on the couple are permitted to begin their life together. The nisu'in is carried out by the ḥuppa and the recitation of the seven blessings of marriage. Because of the great importance of marriage, an additional cup of wine is poured in honour of the seven blessings of marriage, to express the joy of marriage that bursts all bounds.</p>\n<p><b>The first blessing</b> of the seven blessings is the blessing over the wine: \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who creates the fruit of the vine.\" Admittedly, only at the conclusion of the seven blessings will they drink from the wine, but they open with the blessing over the wine so that all the blessings will be said over the cup of wine; and from the conclusion of the blessing over the wine until the groom and bride drink from it, they must be careful not to speak. After that, in <b>the second blessing</b>, they open with a general praise to Hashem who created everything: \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who has created all things for His glory.\" In <b>the third blessing</b> they continue to a more detailed praise for the creation of man: \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, Creator of man.\" In <b>the fourth blessing</b> the praise becomes more detailed, as thanksgiving for the formation of the man and the woman in the image of God, male and female, so that they can join in love and be fruitful and multiply: \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who created man in His image, in the image of the likeness of His form, and prepared for him from himself an everlasting building. Blessed are You, Hashem, Creator of man.\" Before continuing with the blessings for the groom and bride one cannot forget the Land of Israel; therefore <b>the fifth blessing</b> is devoted to a request for all Israel, that just as Hashem gladdens the groom and bride, so He will cause the Land of Israel to rejoice and will comfort her with the gathering of her children into her in joy: \"May the barren one surely rejoice and exult at the gathering of her children into her (speedily) in joy. Blessed are You, Hashem, who gladdens Zion through her children.\" From this they continue to <b>the sixth blessing</b>, and ask Hashem to gladden the groom and bride as He gladdened Adam and Ḥava in the Garden of Eden: \"Gladden, gladden the beloved companions, as You gladdened Your creature in the Garden of Eden of old. Blessed are You, Hashem, who gladdens groom and bride.\" From this they reach <b>the seventh</b> and last <b>blessing</b>, in which they thank and praise Hashem who created joy, and pray for the joy of the groom and bride together with the redemption of the cities of Yehuda and Jerusalem: \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who created gladness and joy, groom and bride, delight, song, mirth and rejoicing, love and brotherhood, peace and companionship. Speedily, Hashem our God, may there be heard in the cities of Yehuda and in the streets of Jerusalem the sound of gladness and the sound of joy, the voice of the groom and the voice of the bride, the sound of the jubilation of grooms from their ḥuppa and of young people from their feasts of song. Blessed are You, Hashem, who gladdens the groom with the bride.\" On choosing those who recite the seven blessings, see below in halakha 8.</p>\n<p>After the conclusion of the seven blessings the rabbi who conducts the kiddushin gives the groom wine to drink, and since they are now married, the groom himself gives his bride wine to drink. After that it is good that the parents and other relatives drink from the wine and finish the cup. If they intended to drink from the wine and fulfil their obligation with the blessing over the wine, they need not recite a blessing before they drink; and if they did not intend, they should recite the blessing over the wine.</p>\n<p>At least ten men must be present at the ḥuppa, for the seven blessings of marriage may not be said without a minyan (SA EH 62:4; 34:4).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-04/","title_he":"ד – הגדרת החופה","title_en":"4. The Definition of the Ḥuppa","body_en":"<p>There are many views regarding the definition of the ḥuppa through which the nisu'in takes effect. Some say that the entry of the couple into a special place designated for the marriage ceremony and the recitation of the seven blessings is the entry into the ḥuppa; and it is customary to prepare for this a decorated canopy spread over four poles, and it is called the ḥuppa. And some say that the ḥuppa takes effect by the groom covering the bride's head with the veil, thereby expressing the spreading of his protection over her, to care from now on for her happiness and well-being.</p>\n<p>Admittedly, in practice the groom covers the bride with the veil and both enter the ḥuppa even before the kiddushin and the giving of the ketuba. But as long as the kiddushin have not been done and the ketuba has not been given, the ḥuppa has no meaning; only after the kiddushin and the giving of the ketuba does the ḥuppa come into expression, and the blessings of marriage can be recited, and they become a married couple.</p>\n<p>And some say that the ḥuppa is done by the groom wrapping himself in a tallit and spreading his tallit also over the bride, and after that the seven blessings are recited. And so is the practice of those of Spanish origin; and indeed this act of wrapping in the tallit is done after the kiddushin, so that according to this custom the entry into the ḥuppa is done after the kiddushin.</p>\n<p>And some say that the ḥuppa is the seclusion of the groom and bride in a room belonging to the groom, for this seclusion is the concrete expression of the beginning of married life (Rambam). And some say that the main thing is that they be secluded, and it makes no difference to whom the room belongs, to the groom or the bride; the main thing is that they be alone in a room for the sake of marriage (the view of the Ramban). And some say that to fulfil the ḥuppa there is no need for the groom and bride to be secluded alone in a room; rather the main thing is that the bride enter a room in the groom's possession, and it makes no difference if other people are there too (Ran). And some say that in this respect the decorated ḥuppa too is the entry of the bride into the groom's place (on all these views see SA and Rema EH 55:1; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 4-11).</p>\n<p>And it seems that there is no dispute between these approaches; for indeed the essence of nisu'in is the beginning of their life together for the sake of the mitzva of marriage, with the recitation of the seven blessings of marriage. And they instituted to do this in an honourable ḥuppa in order to publicise the matter and increase the joy; and the ḥuppa too is customarily held in several ways, according to the tradition received in the various communities, with the conclusion of the ḥuppa in their entry into a room designated for them (see Beit Meir siman 55; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 55:14-17).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-05/","title_he":"ה – המנהג למעשה לעדות השונות","title_en":"5. The Practical Custom in the Various Communities","body_en":"<p>The practical custom is to hold the ḥuppa in several ways, and the witnesses must accompany all the stages of the ḥuppa from beginning to end. First the groom goes to the bride and covers her head with the veil. After that the groom is escorted to the ḥuppa, that is, to beneath the canopy spread in honour of the wedding, and then they go back and escort the bride to the ḥuppa. The groom is brought before the bride because it is more fitting and honourable that the groom wait for the bride and not the bride for the groom. In addition, the entry into the ḥuppa is considered as the entry of the bride into the groom's home, and thereby he takes upon himself the responsibility to care for her happiness and well-being; therefore the groom waits for her at the ḥuppa. When the bride approaches the ḥuppa, it is fitting that the groom step one or two steps towards her to greet her. After both are standing under the ḥuppa, the groom betroths the bride and gives her her ketuba, and the seven blessings of marriage are recited.</p>\n<p>Regarding the stage of yiḥud the customs differ. According to the custom of Ashkenaz and Yemen, they are careful to hold the yiḥud close to the seven blessings of marriage; therefore after the conclusion of the ḥuppa the groom and bride are secluded alone for a few minutes in a closed room, and thereby they begin their life together. And since in the view of the Rambam the room must belong to the groom, together with renting the hall they rent the yiḥud room to the groom, so that it will be considered his that evening. And there must be testimony of the yiḥud; therefore the witnesses must see the couple entering the yiḥud room and staying in it a few moments, and with this the ḥuppa is concluded. And since the blessings of marriage recited under the ḥuppa apply to the groom and bride being secluded in the yiḥud room, one should not interrupt between the ḥuppa and the yiḥud room; therefore the guests postpone their 'mazal tov' wishes until after they come out of the yiḥud room.</p>\n<p>According to the Sephardi custom, in addition to covering with the veil and entering the ḥuppa, after the kiddushin the groom wraps himself in a new tallit and recites 'She-heḥeyanu' over it, and after that spreads it also over his bride; this too expresses the spreading of his protection over her and her entry into the ḥuppa (see Ben Ish Ḥai, first year, Shofetim 12). According to the custom of many, immediately after this four people spread his tallit over the groom and bride; and some of those of Spanish origin do not have this custom.</p>\n<p>According to the Sephardi custom, the groom and bride need not enter a closed room after the ḥuppa; rather this seclusion is done in the couple's home after the end of the wedding, and there is no need for special testimony about it.</p>\n<p>Today, because of the arrangements of the wedding, also those who follow the Sephardi custom are accustomed to go after the ḥuppa to a yiḥud room, so that in that time the guests will sit down in their places and eat the first course. But according to the Sephardi custom no witnesses are set up for this, and there is no need to hurry to go immediately after the ḥuppa to the yiḥud room, and one may stop on the way to say 'mazal tov'.</p>\n<p>It is customary to prepare in the yiḥud room food and drink for the groom and bride, so that they can taste something there at ease. And it is right that they take care to wash their hands there and recite the blessing over the bread and begin the wedding meal, for they are the centre of the joy and the meal; and if because of the pressure and tension that there will be afterwards they forget to wash their hands and eat a little, the wedding meal will be impaired.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-06/","title_he":"ו – הרב מסדר הקידושין","title_en":"6. The Rabbi Who Conducts the Kiddushin","body_en":"<p>Although by law a man who betroths a woman in the presence of two valid witnesses – she is betrothed, it is customary to hold the kiddushin in the presence of a rabbi who is the 'mesader kiddushin', so that he will be responsible for the kiddushin being conducted according to halakha, and no doubts will arise regarding the validity of the kiddushin. Some hold that already in the period of the Talmud it was customary to hold the kiddushin in the presence of a rabbi as 'mesader kiddushin' (Keneset Yeḥezkel siman 72), and some hold that only from the period of the Rishonim did they institute betrothing in the presence of a rabbi as 'mesader kiddushin' (based on the responsa of the Rambam 156).</p>\n<p>The rabbi who conducts the kiddushin must be expert in these laws, as the Sages said (Kiddushin 6a): \"Whoever does not know the nature of gittin and kiddushin should have no dealings with them.\" For kiddushin and divorce are fateful for the status of the couple and their children, and therefore a person who is not expert in the laws of marriage may not conduct the kiddushin and the ḥuppa. The rabbi who conducts the kiddushin must check that the couple are fit to marry each other, lest one of them is a mamzer or not Jewish, or the groom is a kohen and the bride a divorcee, or one of them is already married and may not marry before divorcing his first spouse. And if they are not known, they must bring witnesses that they are indeed unmarried and fit to marry. Today these clarifications are carried out by the rabbis in the religious council who are in charge of the registration of marriages.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-07/","title_he":"ז – האם צריך לשלם עבור סידור הקידושין?","title_en":"7. Must One Pay for Conducting the Kiddushin?","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> Must the couple pay money to the rabbi who conducts the ḥuppa and kiddushin for them? And who has the right to choose the rabbi, the groom or the bride?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> In the past rabbis did not receive a full salary from the communities in which they served in holy office; rather the main part of their salary was collected from the public for the work of the rabbinate, and they would set what sum would be paid for each ḥuppa, and what sum for supervising each slaughter. In those times they were careful that only the rabbi of the community would conduct the ḥuppot in the community, so that his livelihood would not be harmed. And if the members of the family wanted another rabbi to conduct their ḥuppa, that rabbi was obligated to collect from them the customary payment and transfer it to the local rabbi.</p>\n<p>But today there is no need to pay the rabbi who conducts the ḥuppa and kiddushin, because the rabbis receive their salaries through the religious councils, and there is no need to supplement their pay by payment for conducting ḥuppot. Moreover, the enactment of the Rabbinate is that every person who registers for marriage pays a fee for the handling of his marriage, including for conducting the ḥuppa and kiddushin, and the local rabbi may not collect money for conducting the ḥuppa. Only the travel to the wedding must the couple finance for the rabbi. Admittedly, all this is on condition that the one who conducts the ḥuppa is the local rabbi; but if the members of the family ask another rabbi to conduct the ḥuppa, since he is not the local rabbi, this is not his job, and therefore he is entitled to collect from the family a payment for the time he devotes to them. To prevent misunderstandings, it is very desirable that before the wedding they agree with him exactly how much they must pay for conducting the ḥuppa. And if the sum seems high to them, they can turn to the local Rabbinate, which will take care of arranging the ḥuppa and kiddushin without payment, and they can also turn to another rabbi who will conduct their ḥuppa for a sum acceptable to them.</p>\n<p>As a rule, it is right to prefer the local rabbi, because it is important that a permanent connection be created between the rabbi and the members of his community, so that they can be helped by his advice in good days and hard days; and this connection is created and strengthened through his participation in family weddings. By contrast, when one takes a rabbi who became famous for a fine outward appearance, it is almost clear that no connection will be kept between him and the members of the family. In addition, a rabbi is measured by his spiritual and moral level, and not always is the one who became famous for a fine outward appearance indeed the most fitting rabbi to conduct the ḥuppa. Besides this, why pay money for the ḥuppa in addition to the fee that everyone pays anyway, when one can ask this of the local rabbi, who will be glad to conduct the ḥuppa for free? The general rule is that it is fitting for every person to choose to conduct his ḥuppa the rabbi most connected to him and to his family. Usually this is the local rabbi. Admittedly, yeshiva students usually honour their rosh yeshiva with conducting the ḥuppa, because he is the rabbi with whom they are most connected, and through conducting the ḥuppa the connection between them will grow further and strengthen.</p>\n<p>When the families of the groom and bride disagree about the choice of the rabbi, it is the groom's right to determine who the rabbi will be, for in fact the groom is the one who betroths, and the rabbi comes to help and guide him.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-08/","title_he":"ח – המכובדים והמברכים בחופה","title_en":"8. Those Honoured and Those Who Recite the Blessings at the Ḥuppa","body_en":"<p>The one who conducts the ḥuppa is the one who recites the two blessings of kiddushin, over the wine and over the betrothal, as we learned above (halakha 2).</p>\n<p>After that there is an important honour, and that is <b>the reading of the ketuba</b>. When there is a rabbi present who is fitting for this, he is honoured with reading the ketuba; and if not, the one who conducts the ḥuppa reads the ketuba. Many are accustomed to read the main part of the ketuba without the many details in its middle, so as not to burden the public. Some are accustomed to read the ketuba under the ḥuppa in a Hebrew translation, so that the groom and bride and also the other participants in the wedding will understand the meaning of the ketuba; and some prefer to read in the Aramaic text, according to the tradition that has existed in Israel for more than two thousand years.</p>\n<p>After that the <b>seven blessings</b> of marriage are recited. When the blessings are divided among various honoured people, the first blessing is joined to the second, so that in practice one can honour <b>six</b> honoured people with the seven blessings.</p>\n<p>It is right to say the blessings in a loud voice, so that the groom and bride and eight more men can hear them even without the loudspeaker.</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> Whom is it fitting to honour with the seven blessings at the ḥuppa, rabbis or relatives?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> In the past, in many places the custom was that the rabbi of the place would conduct the kiddushin and recite the blessings of betrothal, and the second rabbi in importance would recite all seven blessings. There is a halakhic advantage in this, for all the blessings are connected to one another, and the fifth and sixth blessings, which do not open with 'Barukh', rest on the blessings that preceded them; therefore it is good to recite all of them in sequence. And some were accustomed to honour the ḥazzan of the community with reciting the seven blessings (see Otzar Ha-Poskim 62:1:8, in the name of the Keneset Ha-Gedola, Yad Aharon and Shulḥan Ha-Ezer).</p>\n<p>In recent generations they wanted to honour more rabbis with reciting the blessings, and therefore they became accustomed to divide the seven blessings among six rabbis. And although there is a halakhic drawback in this, in any case the honour of the Torah is preferable (Yabia Omer 4:7). It is possible that the change came about following the improvement in transport conditions, which led to more guests coming to the wedding, and consequently more rabbis too; and since it is fitting to honour all the rabbis with blessings, they became accustomed to divide the seven blessings among six rabbis.</p>\n<p>Since it has already become customary to divide the seven blessings among several people, even when six rabbis have not come, they became accustomed to honour the elders of the family, such as grandfathers and uncles. Not only this, but in many families the custom has already spread of honouring the grandfathers and uncles, and if they are not honoured, there is a concern that they will be offended. Admittedly, ideally, when there are rabbis present, it is fitting that the members of the family honour the visiting rabbis with reciting the blessings, for this is why they became accustomed to split the recitation of the seven blessings among six honoured people. However, if a great offence may arise from this, it is better to honour the members of the family in order to prevent offence.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-09/","title_he":"ט – צום ביום החתונה","title_en":"9. Fasting on the Wedding Day","body_en":"<p>The custom of those of Ashkenazi origin and some of those of Sephardi origin is that the groom and bride fast on their wedding day, so that they will enter their wedding with seriousness and a sense of responsibility, and not out of eating, drinking and drunkenness.</p>\n<p>Beyond this, it is the last day of the period of being single, the period of searching and wondering, and from this day on one can open a new, clean page. On the day that the groom and bride stand under their ḥuppa, their gaze takes in their whole life, past and future, and they are full of hopes for the future. Therefore this day is especially suited to complete repentance, to decide that from now on they will be good and upright people as far as they are able, will love and gladden each other, will build together an exemplary family, will educate their children in Torah and mitzvot, will honour their parents, give tzedaka, fix times for Torah, keep Shabbatot and festivals, and so on. This is a repentance so deep that even on Yom Kippur, with all the prayers and intentions, one does not succeed in reaching its level. Therefore on the wedding day all of a person's sins are forgiven (Yerushalmi Bikkurim 3:3). So that the repentance will be complete, grooms and brides in Ashkenaz became accustomed to fast.</p>\n<p>As for the Sephardi custom, some are accustomed to fast, some are accustomed that only the groom fasts, and many are accustomed not to fast at all; and it is fitting for every person to follow the custom of his fathers, provided that he takes care to enter the ḥuppa with seriousness, with thoughts of repentance.</p>\n<p>For those accustomed to fast, when the wedding takes place at night, since they completed the fast at the emergence of the stars, they may drink and taste a little before the ḥuppa. But they should not drink strong drink nor eat a great deal, so that they will enter the ḥuppa with seriousness.</p>\n<p>A groom or bride for whom the fast may cause a headache or weakness should, so that they will not arrive at the ḥuppa exhausted, take upon themselves to fast until midday, and after that eat and drink a little as they need.</p>\n\n<p>There are days that the Sages established on which one may not fast, because they are good and joyful days for Israel, and on them, according to all the customs, the groom and bride do not fast. These are the days on which it is forbidden to fast: Rosh Ḥodesh, Ḥanukka, Isru Ḥag of the three pilgrimage festivals, the 15th of Av and the 15th of Shevat, Purim and Purim Katan (that is, the 14th and 15th of Adar I in a leap year).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"On Rosh Ḥodesh Nisan, according to the Rema 573:1 and MB 9, they should fast, since it is the custom of the pious to fast on it; and according to the Peri Megadim, one who is not accustomed always to fast on Rosh Ḥodesh Nisan should not fast on it on the day of his ḥuppa. And see the book Rosh Ḥodesh 14:19. And see there in note 39 that if the wedding is on the night of Rosh Ḥodesh, according to the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan EH 61:21 they should continue to fast until after the ḥuppa even though they enter the night of Rosh Ḥodesh fasting, because the reason for the fast is that they not be drunk. And according to the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 146:1, the fast of grooms always continues only until the emergence of the stars, because it is for atonement and repentance. And so is the practice: if the groom and bride who are fasting feel a need for it, they may drink a little after the emergence of the stars before the ḥuppa, and in a case of need even taste a little."}]},{"slug":"11-05-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-10/","title_he":"י – השושבינים","title_en":"10. The Shushvinim (Escorts)","body_en":"<p>One of the beautiful customs, which in Ashkenaz they were more particular about and which today everyone follows, is the custom that the shushvinim escort the groom and bride on their way to the ḥuppa, as one is accustomed to escort a king and queen, while the rest of those gathered sing and dance around them. The shushvinim are the supporting escorts, and this is the custom: the two shushvinim stand at the sides of the groom, one on his right and one on his left, and escort him on his way to the bride to cover her head with the veil (below 12), and afterwards to the ḥuppa. After that the bride rises from her chair, and the two women shushvinot escort her to the ḥuppa, until she stands at the right of the groom.</p>\n<p>According to the Ashkenazi custom the shushvinim carry candles in their hands as a good sign, for the candle symbolises a person's soul, whose task is to burn and give light, and carrying it expresses a wish that the lives of the couple will be full of light and joy. (According to the Ashkenazi custom the bride and the shushvinot circle the groom seven times, below halakha 13.)</p>\n<p>Originally they were careful to choose as shushvinim righteous people whose match had turned out well and who had children, so that this would be a good sign for the wedding, that it would turn out well. In the course of time it became the custom that the shushvinim are the parents, with the fathers leading the groom and the mothers the bride. For it is fitting to honour them with this, since they raised the groom and bride from infancy to youth, with care and devotion, in the beautiful and the hard moments, and it is fitting that on this great day they escort their children to their ḥuppa. But if the parents of one of the sides were divorced or widowed, as a good sign they became accustomed to appoint in their place another couple from the family or close friends whose marriage is a first marriage. Likewise, when one of the parents has died and the remaining parent has remarried, they became accustomed to choose in their place a couple from the family who are married in a first marriage. However, under the ḥuppa the custom is that the parents stand at the sides of the groom and bride even when they are divorced.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>In recent years, since the custom of the shushvinim has become very public and many parents are hurt by not escorting their children to the ḥuppa, the practice is that if the parents wish, they escort the groom and bride whether they are married in a second marriage or whether they are divorced. And it is good that another couple married in a first marriage who have children be added to them, who walk close to them and are also considered shushvinim.</p>\n<p>In addition to the shushvinim who stand at the sides of the groom and bride, in the past they were accustomed to escort the groom with a large crowd from his home to the ḥuppa, and likewise the bride from her home to the ḥuppa, because the groom and bride on their wedding day are like a king and queen, and in this way they were accustomed to honour them. Today the custom of escorting is held at the wedding venue: when the groom begins to walk to the ḥuppa, the men escort him with song and dances; and likewise when the bride walks to the ḥuppa, the women walk with her with song. But it is not fitting that they dance near the men, because of modesty.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"If the parents of the groom or bride married in a second marriage, and the groom was born to them from the second marriage, the authorities on customs disagree. Some say that it is preferable that they not be the shushvinim, because it is a good sign that the shushvinim be married in a first marriage (Minḥat Yitzḥak); and some say that since they are the parents of the groom and bride and they are continuing in their partnership until now, it is fitting that they be the shushvinim (Shulḥan Ha-Ezer and Seridei Esh; and see Nisu'in Ke-Hilkhatam p. 363). Today everyone is accustomed that the parents escort."}]},{"slug":"11-05-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-11/","title_he":"יא – שינוי מנהג השושבינים","title_en":"11. Changing the Custom of the Shushvinim","body_en":"<p>The accepted custom is that the fathers escort the groom and the mothers the bride. In the order of escorting the groom and bride, the groom's father is on his right and the bride's father on his left; and in escorting the bride, the bride's mother is on her right and the groom's mother on her left. And if they reversed the sides, it does not matter. This is the custom in the Land of Israel and in most communities. Recently, families who immigrated from America are accustomed that the groom's father and mother escort the groom, and the bride's father and mother escort the bride. Some rabbis are very particular to continue the custom of the Land of Israel, and so my teacher Rabbi Avraham Shapira zt\"l used to do: he did not agree to conduct a ḥuppa in which they changed from the custom of the Land of Israel.</p>\n<p>However, in practice, if the parents insist on it, since there is no prohibition in it, one should not prevent them. But ideally it is right to continue the custom of the Land of Israel, that the fathers escort the groom and the mothers the bride, and there is in this a positive expression of the uniting of the two families through the wedding of the children. When in the Land of Israel there is an argument between the two families, one should follow the traditional custom in the Land, according to which the fathers escort the groom and the mothers the bride.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-12/","title_he":"יב – ההינומא","title_en":"12. The Veil","body_en":"<p>One of the moving customs at the wedding is the covering of the bride's face with the veil, with which the wedding ceremony opens. The groom walks to the bride, who sits on her honoured and decorated chair, with the veil on her head, that is, a white net fabric made of several layers that fall to the back of her head. The groom approaches the bride, takes hold with his hands of the upper layer of the veil behind her head, moves it over towards her face and covers her. And so, with the veil covering her whole head including her face, the bride remains until the end of the ḥuppa.</p>\n<p>Several reasons have been given for the custom. First, by covering the bride's head with the veil, the groom expresses the spreading of his protection over his partner, that from now on he takes upon himself responsibility to care for her well-being and happiness. And as we learned (in halakha 4), some say that this is the beginning of the ḥuppa, that is, the entry of the bride into her marriage.</p>\n<p>A further reason is modesty. Modesty emphasises that the bride's outward beauty is not her main virtue; rather her inner qualities, hidden in her soul, are her main virtue and the reason for her marriage. Concealing her face moves the emphasis from outward beauty to inner beauty.<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>The custom of the veil is widespread among all those of Ashkenazi origin and most of those of Sephardi origin, but some of those of Sephardi origin are not accustomed to cover the bride's face with the veil.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"A further reason: by the bride's face being covered with the veil, the kiddushin are certainly valid, because there is no concern that perhaps the bride valued the ring at more than its worth and on that understanding was betrothed, so that her kiddushin would turn out to be in error. For since her face is covered, she does not look closely at the ring, and thereby shows her intention that she wants to be betrothed in any case, and it does not matter to her how much exactly the ring is worth (Rema EH 31:2)."}]},{"slug":"11-05-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-13/","title_he":"יג – מנהג שבע ההקפות למנהג אשכנז","title_en":"13. The Custom of the Seven Circuits according to the Ashkenazi Custom","body_en":"<p>According to the Ashkenazi custom, after the bride enters the ḥuppa, together with the shushvinot she circles the groom seven times, and after that stands at his right side. The seven circuits are meant to express that the marriage is for the sake of Heaven; corresponding to the Holy One, blessed be He, being revealed in seven heavens, the bride circles the groom seven times.</p>\n<p>The deep reason is that wherever circuits are made, they allude to a level great and higher than our understanding. This is the difference between the level of the line and the level of the circle. A straight line has a beginning and an end, which expresses our ability to contemplate systematically every linear idea, in which there is a first premise from which a further premise follows, up to the concluding premise. But a circle has no beginning and no end, to allude that this is an infinite idea that we cannot grasp in its entirety, but it has an enormous influence on us, for it surrounds our whole consciousness and gives it inspiration. Speech is alluded to by the line, and what is beyond speech is alluded to by the circle. Therefore when we complete the Torah on Simḥat Torah we are accustomed to make circuits, to allude that after all we have merited to learn and understand, we have still not come to grasp even a tiny bit of the Divine wisdom in the Torah; but since we have merited to engage in it, it surrounds us and we merit to receive from it inspiration that directs our thoughts and elevates our way. And so too at the time of the wedding: the emotion is so great, and the meaning of marriage so deep and sublime, that even all the blessings and words said at the wedding cannot express the greatness of its meaning; and for this they make circuits, in order to express what is above and beyond words and to receive inspiration from it.</p>\n<p>Seven circuits are made because the number seven is the complete number that appears in this world, for the world was created in seven days. Therefore at the completion of the Torah seven circuits are held, to express that the Torah illuminates all seven aspects of this world. And likewise the bride's seven circuits express the indwelling of the holiness of the wedding over all seven aspects of life.</p>\n<p>And the bride circles the groom in order to express, on the one hand, her longing for him, and on the other hand, to allude that from the inner side the level of the woman is higher than that of the man; and therefore in her is expressed the circle and the encompassing that alludes to what is beyond comprehension. Whereas the groom is in the aspect of the line, and therefore he is the one who does the revealed things: he says the formula of kiddushin and he transfers to the bride the ring of kiddushin (see above 4:9-13).</p>\n<p>According to the Sephardi custom, the virtue of the woman's circles is expressed by the groom betrothing the bride with a round ring.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-14/","title_he":"יד – מנהגי החופה","title_en":"14. The Customs of the Ḥuppa","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva to take part in the ḥuppa and thereby to honour the groom and bride, and there is in this also honour of Heaven, for at their ḥuppa the groom and bride fulfil the mitzva of marrying by ḥuppa and kiddushin. So that their standing around the ḥuppa will honour the wedding, those gathered must stand in silence and not chatter.</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> Is it right to arrange chairs for those gathered in honour of the ḥuppa so that they can sit, or is it preferable that they stand, and therefore one should not arrange chairs?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Several of the great Aḥaronim wrote that the public should stand at the time of the ḥuppa, in honour of the groom and bride, who are like a king and queen, and when they are standing it is fitting for everyone to stand. Further, the Shekhina rests at the time of the ḥuppa, and the wedding is a joy of all Israel, and therefore it is fitting to stand in honour of the Shekhina and in honour of Israel. And so it is fitting to do ideally; however, one for whom it is hard may sit (Be'er Heitev EH 62:1, in the name of the Keneset Ha-Gedola; Otzar Ha-Poskim 62:18:8). Therefore it is preferable not to arrange chairs around the ḥuppa, but only to prepare a number of chairs for the elderly and the sick who do not have the strength to stand. And one who has the strength to stand for part of the time should preferably stand while the blessings of betrothal and marriage are recited.</p>\n<p>According to the custom of those of Ashkenazi origin the ḥuppa is set up under the open sky, as a sign of blessing that the descendants of the groom and bride will be like the stars of the heavens (Rema EH 61:1). And according to the custom of those of Sephardi origin the ḥuppa is held in a comfortable and honourable place, usually inside the hall. When it rains, those of Ashkenazi origin too may hold the ḥuppa inside the hall, as is the custom of those of Sephardi origin. Admittedly, there are Ashkenazim who are very particular about keeping the custom, and even when it rains they hold the ḥuppa under the open sky. But since members of all the communities live together today, in a pressing situation those of Ashkenazi origin may follow the customs of those of Sephardi origin, since everyone recognises it alike.</p>\n<p>It is the custom of Israel from generations past that brides wear white dresses, so that there will be no difference between the rich and the poor (see Ta'anit 26b). In continuation of this, it is fitting for the groom and bride to act with modesty according to the tradition of Israel, and to avoid trying to create an ostentatious ceremony, so as not to become proud. Likewise it is fitting not to hold a unique ceremony, out of concern for the evil eye; for in any case the groom and bride stand at the centre of attention, and if they make themselves stand out more, they will cause their deeds to be scrutinised and bring accusation upon themselves. For there are people whose lot has not been kind to them: there are poor grooms and brides, there are single men and women who long to find their match, there are widowers and widows, divorced men and women; and therefore it is fitting for them to be modest on their great day and not make themselves stand out excessively.</p>\n<p>Likewise it is fitting for the groom and bride not to express their love for each other publicly at the time of the ḥuppa, for modesty is becoming to their love, so that it will flourish and rise over long days and years. And they should not speak at the time of the ḥuppa with each other nor with any person; rather they should listen with complete concentration to all the blessings, and intend and pray that all the blessings will be fulfilled in them.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-15/","title_he":"טו – זכר לחורבן","title_en":"15. A Remembrance of the Destruction","body_en":"<p>Precisely at the time of the wedding, at the height of joy, one must remember that there is still sorrow in the world: the Temple is destroyed and the people of Israel do not yet dwell in its land as is fitting for it, and all the troubles in the world stem from this; and consequently it is not possible to rejoice with complete joy. Only after we remember the Temple and all the sorrow in the world can we rejoice with true joy. Without this our joy will be false, full of frivolity that cuts a person off from real life. But when we remember Jerusalem, we show thereby that the wedding is directed at the repair of the world, and consequently it is considered as the building of one of the ruins of Jerusalem. In this way the joy is true.</p>\n<p>It is told of Rav Ashi that he made a wedding for his son, and saw that the young students were rejoicing at the wedding too much, and he broke a white glass cup before them, in order to remind them of the destruction of the Temple (Berakhot 31a). This custom is widespread today throughout Israel, to break a glass under the ḥuppa, in remembrance of the destruction of the Temple. Some are accustomed to break the glass immediately after the kiddushin, that is, after giving the ring. And some are accustomed to break the glass after the conclusion of the seven blessings, at the end of the ḥuppa. And some wonder at this, because it turns out that the ḥuppa ends with the breaking of the glass, and immediately afterwards the public cheers 'mazal tov' – and what place is there for the jubilation of 'mazal tov' after mentioning the destruction?! But my father and teacher too is accustomed so, and explains that indeed it is fitting that at the end of the ḥuppa the joy reach its peak and everyone say 'mazal tov', except that then, at the height of joy, one must stop to remember Jerusalem and the sorrow of the world, and after we have remembered this we may rejoice with true joy. Therefore immediately after remembering Jerusalem, the joy that was held back bursts out again.</p>\n<p>To emphasise that the breaking of the glass is meant in remembrance of Jerusalem, it is fitting that the groom say before breaking the glass the verse (Tehillim 137:5-6): \"If I forget you, O Jerusalem, let my right hand forget its skill; let my tongue cleave to my palate if I do not remember you, if I do not set Jerusalem above my chief joy.\" Today many are accustomed that the band plays these verses and many join it in song, and then there is no need for the groom to repeat the verses.</p>\n<p>The Sages instituted as a remembrance of the destruction that before the groom goes out to the ḥuppa, ashes are placed on his head, in the place where the head tefillin are placed (SA EH 65:3). And so many are accustomed. But on the other hand many are not accustomed to place ashes on the groom's head (Kaf Ha-Ḥayim OḤ 560:21). And they raise a difficulty against them: the Sages instituted placing ashes on a groom's head, and how can one cancel their enactment? But in their view the essence of the enactment is to recall the destruction of Jerusalem and the Temple, and since this is already recalled by breaking the glass, there is no further need to place ashes on the groom's head. Ideally it is good to keep both customs, to place ashes and to break a glass; and those who wish to make do with breaking the glass may do so.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-16/","title_he":"טז – שמחת החתונה","title_en":"16. The Joy of the Wedding","body_en":"<p>The joy of the wedding is the most joyful event within Jewish life. Countless melodies and songs have been composed around the wedding, because the joy of the wedding is one of the foundations of Judaism. Unlike members of various religions who saw marriage as a surrender to man's base inclination, Judaism, out of its positive approach to life, sees the wedding as the greatest and most joyful day in a person's life. It is the day on which love comes to fulfilment in full, and on which the foundation is laid for the future generations who will be born, with Hashem's help, to the new couple. And all those gathered try to take part in the dancing, because \"it is a great mitzva to gladden groom and bride and to dance before them\" (Tur EH 65). The Sages greatly emphasised the importance of the mitzva and said (Berakhot 6b): Whoever benefits from the meal of a groom and does not gladden him transgresses the five voices, for five voices are mentioned in the verse that deals with the joy of the wedding, as it is said (Yirmeyahu 33:11): \"The voice of gladness and the voice of joy, the voice of the groom and the voice of the bride, the voice of those who say: Give thanks to the Lord of hosts.\" And if he gladdens him, what is his reward? Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: He merits the Torah, which was given with five voices. And Rabbi Abbahu said: It is as if he offered a thanksgiving offering. And Rav Naḥman bar Yitzḥak said: It is as if he rebuilt one of the ruins of Jerusalem.</p>\n<p>Seemingly one should ask: why do the groom and bride need people to dance before them and gladden them – they are glad anyway, and perhaps they would even prefer to be alone together? The answer is that admittedly the groom and bride do not need to be gladdened, but by gladdening them their joy becomes greater and more important. They understand that there is enormous value to their wedding and to their decision to build a family in Israel, and this strengthens and elevates their love for each other and gives them great strength for their whole life.</p>\n<p>Through the joy of the wedding, the private joy of the couple becomes a joy of all Israel, which reveals the inner unity in the world, expressed in the uniting of the two families. And out of this new life will come into the world, to the glory of the Torah, the people and the land. By virtue of raising love to its general dimension, one may hope that the private love between the couple will grow and strengthen over the years, and will not fade as the small physical loves do.</p>\n<p>To increase the joy, it is a mitzva to hire a band for the wedding. It once happened that the wife of the governor of a certain province in Ashkenaz died, and the governor ordered that no musical instrument be played for the whole year of mourning for her; and the time came for a groom and bride to marry, and they asked the Maharil how to act. The Maharil ruled that they should not hold a wedding without musical instruments, and if it is impossible for them to play in their place, they should hold the wedding in another place, and there bring musical instruments and play for the joy of the wedding (MA OḤ 338:4).</p>\n<p>And if one side does not want to increase the joy, the other side may compel it to hire a band in order to increase the joy (Ranaḥ, Keneset Ha-Gedola, glosses on the Tur EH 64:2). One may hire a DJ instead of a band.</p>\n<p>One must take care that the band not be too loud, because the noise of the band causes suffering to many and even damages the sense of hearing. This problem arose in the last generation, after the development of the loudspeaker and the possibility of raising the volume with a slight movement. But those in charge of the band must remember that their role is to add joy and not to cause distress to the guests; and the foundation of the whole Torah is not to cause people distress, and what is hateful to you do not do to your fellow (Shabbat 31a). It is further fitting to suggest that during the meal the band not play at all, and so the guests will be able to rest a little from the loud sounds during the dancing. Also one who wishes to add a little background music during the meal should make sure that the guests can talk with each other comfortably, for the pleasant conversations between those gathered are part of the joy of the wedding.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-17/","title_he":"יז – כיצד מרקדים לפני הכלה","title_en":"17. How One Dances before the Bride","body_en":"<p>Beit Hillel said that when one dances before the bride, one should praise her beauty and her qualities and say that she is \"a beautiful and gracious bride,\" for through this the groom's love for her will increase. And even when the bride is not considered beautiful and gracious in the eyes of most people, one should praise her so, because presumably in her husband's eyes she is beautiful and gracious, and his joy grows when those gathered also praise her so (Ketubot 17a).<sup>5</sup></p>\n<p>From here we have learned that, within the limits of modesty, the relatives and friends of the groom should praise the bride before him; and the more they praise her with things that are acceptable to him, the more they merit to fulfil the mitzva of gladdening groom and bride, for out of the groom's joy in his bride he gladdens her more. And likewise it is a mitzva for the relatives and friends of the bride to praise her groom before her, so that through this she will rejoice in him more. And so the Sages said (Berakhot 6b): \"The reward for attending a wedding is the words.\" That is, the reward that the participants in the wedding receive is for the words of praise with which they praise the bride before the groom and the groom before the bride, and for the words of blessing with which they bless the groom and bride.</p>\n<p>\"They said of Rabbi Yehuda bar Ilai that he would take a sprig (branch) of myrtle and dance before the bride, and say: A beautiful and gracious bride.\" And presumably, from the very fact that one as great in Torah as he set aside his learning to dance before the bride, the bride understood how great her worth is and how great the value of the home she is about to build, and she was left with a wonderful memory of her wedding, which even in hard hours breathed joy and encouragement into her (Ketubot 17a).</p>\n<p>Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzḥak was one of the great Amoraim, and even when he was old he used to dance before the bride while holding three myrtle branches, throwing and catching them alternately, until the young people were ashamed before him that they could not dance and gladden the groom and bride as he did. When he died, a pillar of fire came down and separated between him and the people, and it was a tradition among the Sages that this pillar of fire comes down only for one or two in a generation. The Sages said that Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzḥak merited his special distinction by virtue of the myrtles with which he would dance before the bride. And some explained that he merited his special distinction because he persisted in doing so before every bride. And some explained that he merited his special distinction because he would act the fool and demean himself in order to gladden the bride (Ketubot 17a).</p>\n<p>From all this we have learned how great and immense is the mitzva to dance at a wedding before the groom and bride, and to gladden them and honour them with this.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"However, Beit Shammai said: and what if the bride is lame or blind – must one then too praise her as \"beautiful and gracious\"? In their view one may not praise her for something that is not in her, and if the bride is unattractive, it is better that they find some other praiseworthy thing in her to mention, and not say something false. However, even according to Beit Shammai, if one of the family members comes at the wedding and asks about the bride's beauty and graciousness, one should praise her, for it is a mitzva to deviate from the truth for the sake of peace (Yevamot 65b). But if they were not asked, in their view it is better not to say things that are not true (Tosafot Ketubot 17a, s.v. 'kalla'). And the halakha follows Beit Hillel, that in any case one should praise her beauty, for certainly from a certain aspect, and that is the groom's aspect, the bride is beautiful and gracious, and those gathered should gladden him and strengthen him in this."}]},{"slug":"11-05-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-18/","title_he":"יח – הסעודה","title_en":"18. The Meal","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva to hold an important meal at a marriage, and this meal should be greater and more joyful than a Yom Tov meal, and a Yom Tov meal is greater and more joyful than a Shabbat meal. At the wedding meal, in addition to bread, one should serve meat and wine, which bring joy. Poultry too is considered joyful, for we find in the Talmud (Ketubot 5a) that they would prepare fowl for a wedding. Families who love and rejoice more in dairy foods may prepare a dairy meal, provided that the dishes are especially fine and good, in order to express the importance of the wedding meal, which is more important than all meals.</p>\n<p>The Sages instituted that preparations for the meal be made for three days, for the honour of the daughters of Israel (Ketubot 2a). This was at a time when the women of the groom's family prepared the meal. But today, when one hires a caterer, there is no need for the family members to toil for three days over its preparation; rather it is enough that they see to it that the meal is honourable. If the groom does not want to make a meal as is customary, the bride's family may compel him to make a meal as is fitting for them, according to his honour and their honour (Igrot Moshe YD 2:112).</p>\n<p>It is a great mitzva to send invitations to acquaintances who are poor and lonely, to widows and orphans, and to urge them to come to the wedding. And so we have learned in the Torah, that whenever a person rejoices in his success he must share his joy with the poor and with converts. So on the festivals (Devarim 16:14), on bringing vow offerings (Devarim 12:12), second tithe (Devarim 14:26), and bringing first fruits (Devarim 26:11). Therefore, when a person draws up his list of invitees, he must think first of the poor, the widows and the embittered among his acquaintances. If he forgets to invite ordinary friends no disaster will happen; one can explain to them that he forgot. But if he forgets the poor and the widows, bitter thoughts will flood their hearts. At first they will still hope that perhaps the invitation is delayed. But when the days pass, the time of the celebration goes by and the invitation does not arrive, they will understand that they no longer count, and their pain and grief will intensify, and the accusation against the wedding will grow. They must not be forgotten. Only after they have been included in the joy can one merit true joy, which adds goodness and blessing to the world.</p>\n<p>In order to hold the meal with fitting splendour there is no need to sink into debt and spend excessively, for one can hold a joyful wedding at a lower cost than is customary, provided that one takes care to serve meat and wine, which bring joy. Therefore it is fitting to encourage the public to hold joyful and modest weddings. Every person and every circle according to their situation, in such a way that the wedding meal is more important and honourable than all the other meals they are accustomed to hold during the year, but not with excess meant to show off and be proud.</p>\n<p>Wealthy people who live in wealth and spend large sums on luxuries should make expensive weddings for their children. For it cannot be that they spend vast fortunes on other luxuries and save on the wedding, until the wedding meal is less than the meals they are accustomed to hold on Shabbatot and in restaurants. On the contrary, they are obligated to spend much more on their children's weddings, in order to raise the value of the mitzva of marriage and its holiness above all other luxuries and mundane matters, and they should include in their joy Torah scholars and the poor. And this is the modesty required of the wealthy: that they do this for the sake of Heaven and not in order to show off their great wealth before their friends.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-19/","title_he":"יט – האם מותר לצאת באמצע חתונה","title_en":"19. May One Leave in the Middle of a Wedding?","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> I was invited to the wedding of a friend from yeshiva. I am already married, and I want to leave in the middle of the wedding in order to return home early. Is this proper? <b>Answer:</b> If you are sitting on a chair, and they are paying for you as for one who ate a full portion, and you are not giving a gift equal to the price of the portion and are not dancing throughout the dancing, then you are included in what the Sages said (Berakhot 6b): \"Whoever benefits from the meal of a groom and does not gladden him transgresses the five voices\" (because five voices are mentioned in the verse that deals with the joy of the wedding).</p>\n<p>However, friends and family members who take part in the wedding and give a monetary gift equal in value to the price of the portion, even if they do not dance, are not considered as benefiting from the groom's meal without gladdening him, because they are taking part in financing their share. Admittedly, they too have a great mitzva to gladden the groom and bride and to remain until the end of the wedding, but if they need to leave, they commit no transgression. But the friends of the groom and the bride are invited in order to dance and gladden, and for this the families of the groom and bride pay a considerable sum for their portions; and whoever of them leaves before the end of the dancing commits a transgression, for he benefits from the groom's meal and does not gladden him, and thereby impairs the honour of the groom and bride, that there is not enough dancing at their celebration. And the same law applies to all other friends and relatives who do not pay the price of the portion.</p>\n<p>When it concerns a close friend who, because of circumstances beyond his control, must leave in the middle of the wedding, and nevertheless the groom agrees and wants him to take part in his joy and his meal, the friend may take part in the meal and leave in the middle. But if many do so, the joy of the wedding will be impaired.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-20","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-20/","title_he":"כ – יציאה לפני ברכת המזון ושבע ברכות","title_en":"20. Leaving before Birkat Ha-Mazon and the Seven Blessings","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> May one who gives a gift equal to the price of the portion, or for whom it is clear that the groom and bride are willing for him to leave in the middle, leave before Birkat Ha-Mazon? Seemingly, even when he is not stealing from the groom's meal, because of his obligation to take part in the zimun and the seven blessings he must stay at least until the end of Birkat Ha-Mazon?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The obligation on the individual to stay with his companions until Birkat Ha-Mazon with zimun applies in an ordinary situation; but in a case of necessity or great need, one may separate from the group and recite the blessing without zimun. For example, one who is concerned that if he stays until the end of the wedding he will have difficulty getting up the next day for prayer or will be tired at his work, or who has a fixed study session and if he stays until the end of the wedding he will lose the session. All these and the like may recite the blessing without zimun. However, one who has no great need, since he joined together with everyone at the meal, is obligated to stay until the zimun and the seven blessings.</p>\n<p>And one who knows in advance that he will have to leave before the end of the meal, so that it will be clear that he does not become obligated in the zimun, should resolve in his mind that he is not joining with them fully, and it is desirable that he even tell his neighbours that he will have to leave before the end. In any case, if two of his neighbours at the table want to join him for zimun, he should make zimun with them quietly and say in the zimun 'she-ha-simḥa bi-me'ono', but he should not make zimun over a cup of wine, and all the more so should not make zimun with ten, so as not to impair the honour of the meal. And it is good that those who join the zimun afterwards eat another ke-zayit, so that they will be true partners in the zimun of ten at the end of the meal (Peninei Halakha, Berakhot 5:11).</p>\n<p>When it concerns honoured people such as rabbis whose time is precious, and the groom wants them to make zimun with ten, or the groom knows that the meal is long and agrees that they leave in the middle, it is right that those leaving make zimun with ten and even recite the seven blessings. And if it is hard for them to gather ten, they should make zimun with three over the cup and recite the seventh blessing – 'Asher bara'.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"In the book Yosef Ometz 159 he wrote that even the loss of much time, for one for whom this is hard, permits reciting the blessing without zimun. And therefore at a wedding, although the need to leave is not a necessity, if it concerns much time, one may be lenient even for an ordinary need. And presumably he meant a person who paid the price of the portion as was customary. But on the other hand one must remember that there is a mitzva to stay until the end of the wedding meal, and some have said that the words of the Sages (Berakhot 6b), \"Whoever benefits from the meal of a groom and does not gladden him transgresses the five voices,\" are directed at one who does not wait until the seven blessings (R. Ḥayim of Brisk, as brought in Nit'ei Gavriel 98:1). Therefore one should not be lenient in this without a real need. However, whoever may separate from the zimun may separate before the seven blessings, for the obligation in them is not greater than the obligation in zimun (see Otzar Ha-Poskim 62:25:10).<br>One who leaves in the middle of the meal should not recite the blessing over the cup, so as not to impair the honour of the meal, and likewise should not make zimun with ten, because of the hurt to the groom and bride that a whole group separates in the middle of the meal (see SA 193:1); but he should say 'she-ha-simḥa bi-me'ono' (see Sha'arei Ha-Berakha 5, note 96). However, if the groom or bride want them to make zimun, for example if they are rabbis, then they should make zimun over the cup and say the seven blessings. And we find similarly what the SA wrote in EH 62:11, that there may be at the wedding meal several groups that recite the seven blessings (and as brought in Otzar Ha-Poskim 62:22:10 in the name of Ḥeshev Ha-Efod 9). And it is evident from the SA there that there is no need for the groom to sit with them, for one may recite the seven blessings at a groom's meal even when he does not hear the blessings; and so wrote the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 62:37."}]},{"slug":"11-05-21","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-21/","title_he":"כא – המתנות","title_en":"21. The Gifts","body_en":"<p>It is right that the invited guests give a monetary gift worth a little more than the value of the portion, and so many of the communities of the East and North Africa are accustomed. And so was the ancient custom in Israel, so that through this the families of the groom and bride will be strengthened in holding the celebration and in helping the couple (Bava Batra 144b; SA EH 60:1).<sup>7</sup></p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> With us it is accepted to pay the price of the portion, but many of my family members hold very expensive weddings, when a portion costs two hundred shekels and more, and sometimes there are three weddings in a month. Although I work and earn a reasonable salary, I am not able to cover the price of these portions and also support my family. I can give gladly to each wedding two hundred shekels for my wife and me, but not three hundred or four hundred shekels. Would it not be fitting to abolish this custom, which creates great difficulty for families blessed with children? <b>Answer:</b> The custom of giving a gift a little more than the value of the portion is a correct custom at its foundation, and so Israel has done from days of old, as explained in the Talmud. And this is on condition that the price of the portion is reasonable. The problem is that recently a drift has been created, and some spend sums that are too high, many times in order to show off. The solution is not in abolishing the custom but in speaking openly about the problem. Immediately after you receive an invitation, call the hosts and tell them that you will be glad to take part in their wedding, but if the portion costs more than a hundred shekels you will not be able to take part, because you cannot cover the price of the portion you will eat. If nevertheless they urge you to take part, you can take part while giving the gift you intended to give. And if they do not urge you, it does not matter; do not take part. If many speak about this openly, in the course of time wedding prices will return to a reasonable level.</p>\n<p>In communities and congregations where there is no binding custom to pay the price of the portion, one for whom it is hard to pay the price of the portion may give a gift as is customary among them, for the hosts prepare in advance for the gifts not covering the cost of the wedding. The problem is that at many weddings today people with different customs take part, and so a problem may arise: the hosts will expect the invited guests to cover the cost of the wedding, and some of the guests will give small gifts, as they themselves received at their own wedding and at their children's weddings. The problem grows in families blessed with children, and grows more when the hosts rent a hall where the portions are expensive. Therefore it is fitting that anyone who has difficulty paying the price of the portion speak with the hosts and clarify whether they expect him to pay the price of the portion; and if so, he should announce in advance that he will not be able to take part in the wedding. And only if nevertheless they urge him to come, he should come and give as he is able.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Bava Batra 144b–145a, Tur and Beit Yosef EH siman 60; and so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (EH 60:1): When a man marries a woman, it is the custom that his friends and acquaintances send him money, so that he will be strengthened for the expense he incurs at the feast. And this money is called shushvinut. And this shushvinut is not a complete gift, for this one sent it to him only on condition that if he marries a woman, he will in turn send him as he sent him. Therefore, if this one married a woman and he did not return the shushvinut to him, he may sue him in court and recover it from him… However, the Shulḥan Arukh concludes: \"And since in our time it is not the custom to make this claim, I have seen fit not to elaborate on this.\""}]},{"slug":"11-05-22","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-22/","title_he":"כב – שבת חתן","title_en":"22. Shabbat Ḥatan","body_en":"<p>The Ashkenazi custom is to call the Shabbat before the wedding 'Shabbat Ḥatan', and to call the groom up to the Torah and hold a celebration in his honour, because the blessing of the whole week is hidden in and flows from Shabbat, and therefore already on Shabbat the joy of the wedding is evident. And so too the Sephardim were accustomed in the past (Rashba in Mishmeret Ha-Bayit). But today they are not accustomed to this, apparently because they do not want to hold a celebration without the groom and bride taking part in it together. However, according to the Sephardi custom too, they try to call the groom up to the Torah.</p>\n<p>On the Shabbat after the wedding all the communities are accustomed to call the groom up to the Torah and to increase the joy of the meals, for this Shabbat is within the seven days of feasting, and if so the meals of this Shabbat become also 'sheva berakhot' meals. Those of Sephardi origin are accustomed to call this Shabbat 'Shabbat Ḥatan', after the aliya to the Torah given to the groom. And those of Ashkenazi origin are accustomed to call this Shabbat 'Shabbat Sheva Berakhot', because according to their custom 'Shabbat Ḥatan' is the Shabbat before the wedding.</p>\n<p>The custom of being called up to the Torah on the Shabbat before the wedding comes to teach that all the elevation and progress the groom has in the wedding comes from the power of the Torah; and therefore out of the groom's ascent to the Torah on the Shabbat before the wedding he continues to his wedding. And the aliya to the Torah on the Shabbat after the wedding comes to show that by virtue of the wedding the groom can continue and rise in spiritual levels; and therefore immediately after the wedding he goes up to the Torah. The groom's aliya on 'Shabbat Ḥatan' takes precedence over the obligations of a yahrzeit, and only one who has reached the age of mitzvot on that day is equal in rank to the groom. Some are accustomed to call the groom up for maftir, and some are accustomed for the third or the last. Ideally the groom's father and the bride's father are also called up to the Torah.</p>\n<p>Some are accustomed to escort the groom to the synagogue for Shaḥarit with songs, and it is a beautiful custom, especially when the groom sleeps in a separate apartment; for if they do not come to escort him, there is a concern that because of tiredness he will not wake up in time for prayer, and afterwards they will have to rush him hurriedly to the synagogue so that he manages to be called up to the Torah. And in any case, even when they are not accustomed to bring him with song, it is good that one of his friends see to waking him in time for prayer.</p>\n<p>Many are accustomed to seat the groom in an honoured place near the Ark (Maḥzor Vitry). They sing in honour of the groom when he goes up to the Torah. After his aliya to the Torah many are accustomed to throw sweets to the children as a good sign, and the source for this is in the Gemara, but one must take care not to throw food that will become repulsive through this (Berakhot 50b). In many places the custom is that the women throw the sweets.</p>\n<p>The custom is that those praying with the groom on 'Shabbat Ḥatan' bless the groom and bride. And the Sages said (Yalkut Shimoni Rut, remez 608) that there is great benefit from this for the groom and bride, and by virtue of the blessings with which they blessed Rut when she married Boaz, the house of David was saved from destruction in the days of the wicked Atalya. And therefore the Sages said that it is a great mitzva of kindness to bless the groom and bride (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 17; Masekhet Soferim 19:12).</p>\n<p>In order to give character to the standing of the groom and bride, and so that they will bless them, the custom is to serve refreshments to those gathered after the Shabbat prayer. In the past there were places, such as Worms, where they were accustomed to hold the celebration on Friday night after the meal. Today the custom is to serve refreshments after Shaḥarit. And perhaps one could suggest that in winter they hold the celebration at night, and in summer after Shaḥarit.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-05-23","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-23/","title_he":"כג – שבעת ימי המשתה","title_en":"23. The Seven Days of Feasting","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva for the groom and bride to rejoice for seven days, and not to do work nor engage in business, but to eat and drink and rejoice together (SA EH 64:1). And this was instituted by Moshe our teacher (Yerushalmi Mo'ed Katan 1:7). Apparently this custom existed among the dignitaries of the nations even before the giving of the Torah, as we find that Lavan the Aramean said to Yaakov our father: \"Fulfil the week of this one,\" and Moshe our teacher saw that it is a correct custom that the groom rejoice with the bride for seven days, and therefore instituted it for Israel (Ramban there).</p>\n<p>The custom is that the groom and bride wear festive clothes throughout the seven days, and they may wash and iron festive clothes for the seven days. It is further the custom that the groom or bride not go out alone into the street of the city, because the groom and bride are like a king and queen, and it is not in keeping with their honour to walk in the street alone. But when they walk together, according to many they do not need an escort. During these seven days the seven blessings are recited at meals at which there is a minyan.</p>\n<p>When the groom is in the synagogue, the congregation does not say Viduy and Taḥanun, as on other days of joy on which Viduy and Taḥanun are not said. And on days on which there is a Torah reading, the groom is called up to the Torah.<sup>8</sup></p>\n<p>Even if for the groom or the bride this is a second marriage, if for one of the couple this is a first marriage, it is a mitzva for them to rejoice for seven days and to recite the seven blessings (SA EH 62:6; 64:2).</p>\n<p>But if it is a second marriage for both the groom and the bride, they have a mitzva to rejoice not for seven days but for only three days. And the seven blessings are recited only at the first meal after the wedding, provided that that meal is on the wedding day itself; so that if the wedding took place before sunset, one must take care to hold the meal before sunset. Since the seven blessings are recited on the first day only, the mitzva to gladden them is only on the first day. And on the second and third days they should rejoice by themselves, and should not go out to work.</p>\n<p>And the same law applies to a couple who were married and divorced and remarried each other, that the seven blessings are recited at the first meal on the wedding day itself. And although their joy is less than an ordinary second marriage, it is a mitzva for them to rejoice for three days (see Otzar Ha-Poskim 62:23).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"There is a doubt how the days are counted: whether part of the day counts as the whole, so that if the wedding was on Sunday before sunset, then at the emergence of the stars the second day already begins, and until sunset on Motza'ei Shabbat one may say the seven blessings; or whether one counts from moment to moment, and the seven days continue until close to sunset on the following Sunday. Regarding the seven blessings, since it is a doubt concerning blessings, one says that part of the day counts as the whole, and the seven blessings are said only until sunset on Motza'ei Shabbat. But regarding Viduy and Taḥanun one follows the view that they are seven full days, and Viduy and Taḥanun are not said on Sunday until close to sunset (MB 131:26; She'erit Yosef vol. 3 p. 132)."}]},{"slug":"11-05-24","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-24/","title_he":"כד – סעודת שבע ברכות","title_en":"24. The Sheva Berakhot Meal","body_en":"<p>During the seven days of feasting, one may recite the seven blessings of the wedding at every meal, provided that a minyan of ten men takes part in the meal, under the conditions explained in the next halakha.</p>\n<p>The purpose of these blessings is to give thanks to Hashem and to bless the groom and bride that they will build their home in joy and love, and within this we thank Hashem for the creation of the world and of man and pray for Jerusalem and the redemption.</p>\n<p>Since these are important blessings that benefit the groom and bride, it is a mitzva to make a meal every day so that they can be recited. But there is no obligation in this. Therefore if they are not able to hold a meal every day, because they do not have money to spend on it and also have no relatives and friends who will make the meals for them, they are not obligated to do so (Rav Pe'alim vol. 4, 6).</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> May one hold a sheva berakhot meal in any home, or only in the groom's home?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Admittedly, it was ruled in the 'Shulḥan Arukh' (EH 62:10), following several of the great Rishonim (Tosafot, Rashba and others), that only in the groom's home are the seven blessings recited, because the groom and bride are more honoured and glad when people come to their home to gladden them.</p>\n<p>But in the view of several Rishonim, one may recite the seven blessings in any place where a meal is made in their honour (Meiri and Ra'avad). In addition, in the view of many of the great Aḥaronim (Yam Shel Shlomo, Taz 62:7), even those who held that the seven blessings may be recited only in the groom's home meant this only according to what was customary in the past, to hold all seven days of feasting in the groom's home, the place where the groom and bride slept and rejoiced; in that place they would hold the meals, and there they would recite the seven blessings. And had they needed to leave their home, there would be in this an impairment of their joy and honour. But today, when this custom has lapsed, the joy is in any place where a meal is held in their honour. And so all the Ashkenazim are accustomed.</p>\n<p>However, regarding the Sephardi custom there is a dispute. Some say that also today one should act as is written in the 'Shulḥan Arukh', that only in the groom's home may the seven blessings be recited; but if a meal is held in another home, the seven blessings are not recited but only the blessing 'Asher bara'. And if an Ashkenazi happens to be there who wants to recite the blessings, one should not prevent him, but one should not invite him for that purpose (Yalkut Yosef, Sova Semaḥot 17:6).</p>\n<p>But most of the Sephardi poskim agreed that in recent generations, when the custom is to hold the celebration of the groom and bride in different homes, consequently the seven blessings are also recited in different homes. And so ruled the Radbaz (vol. 4, siman 1320), and so they ruled in Turkey (Keneset Ha-Gedola, and R. Ḥayim Palagi), and so the Maharitz ruled in Yemen (Pe'ulat Tzaddik 3:252), and so ruled the great rabbis of North Africa, among them Rabbi Khalfon of Djerba (Berit Kehuna) and Rabbi Messas, and so ruled Rabbi Ḥayim David Ha-Levi. And so in practice most Sephardim are accustomed.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In the book 'Alei Hadas' p. 180, which is a collection of the customs of Tunis, he wrote: \"…Most of the rabbis of our region who addressed this law concluded to permit saying the seven blessings outside the groom's home, especially in our days, when a fixed place for the ḥuppa is not set for all seven days. And so ruled Rabbi Kadir Tzaban in his book Nefesh Ḥaya (section Ḥet, item 10), and he wrote at the end of his words: And one should not compare to what they are accustomed abroad, where the groom and bride live with their parents in a large and spacious courtyard and there they fix the meals of the night of the ḥuppa or of Shabbat, for in truth that is called the place of the ḥuppa, end quote. And the gaon Rabbi Meir Mazuz shlit\"a agreed with him in law in a note there. And further, Rabbi Meir Mazuz brought testimony that so in practice acted Rabbi Moshe Khalfon Ha-Kohen, author of Berit Kehuna. And see further Responsa Sho'el Ve-Nish'al (3, EH 438). And so wrote at length the book Teraḥem Tziyon (siman 12). And so is the view of several of the great Aḥaronim, such as: the Radbaz (vol. 4, siman 1320), the Keneset Ha-Gedola (EH 62, Hagahot Ha-Tur 27), the gaon Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi in his book Ru'aḥ Ḥayim (EH 62:11), the gaon Rabbi Yehuda Shmuel Ashkenazi in the siddur Beit Oved (laws of birkat ḥatanim, item 18, folio 200b), and others.\""}]},{"slug":"11-05-25","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-05-25/","title_he":"כה – מהלכות שבע ברכות","title_en":"25. Some Laws of the Sheva Berakhot","body_en":"<p>When ten men take part in a meal during the seven days of feasting, after Birkat Ha-Mazon the seven blessings are recited over a cup of wine. And one of those at the meal must be 'panim ḥadashot' (a new face), that is, a person who has not yet taken part in the celebration of the groom and bride, by virtue of whom the joy increases; and a dish is added for him. And some say that there must be two new people in order to recite the seven blessings (R. Avraham in the name of his father the Rambam), and so some of the Sephardim are accustomed. And the custom of the Ashkenazim and some of the Sephardim is to make do with one who is 'panim ḥadashot', and in a case of need all the Sephardim may act so (see Otzar Ha-Poskim 62:33:2).</p>\n<p>On Shabbat and Yom Tov the seven blessings are recited even without 'panim ḥadashot', because the joy of the day serves in place of the 'panim ḥadashot'.</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> May the seven blessings be recited when not all of the ten ate bread?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> There are different views on this among the Aḥaronim, and the practice is according to the stringent view, that of the ten at least seven must eat bread, and another three must eat at least fruit.<sup>10</sup></p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> May one who did not eat bread or fruit be honoured with reciting one of the seven blessings?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The Aḥaronim disagree about this. Some say that one may honour with a blessing even one who did not himself eat, and some say that only one who ate bread may recite one of the seven blessings. And so it is fitting to act, and one should not protest against those who are lenient, for they have on whom to rely (see Nisu'in Ke-Hilkhatam 14:95).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"There are four views on this: a) According to the lenient view it is enough that three of the ten eat bread (Ha-Elef Lekha Shlomo OḤ 93). b) At least three must eat bread and three eat other foods, even without bread (Minḥat Pittim 62:5). c) Six must eat bread (Zekhor Le-Avraham). d) Seven must eat bread, and another three eat at least fruit. And so is the custom. See the book Nisu'in Ke-Hilkhatam 14:41."}]}]});
